A||||7||RobertVol||Sunroof Repair||||1||
Z||000000||RobertVol||12-11-2000||11:20 PM||||Been gone awhile but had a friend call me the other day. he said that we was having horrible problems with his Sunroof on his 85 GT. He cant seem to get the leak stopped. any suggestions? anyone know a good repair person for sunroofs in fieros in the state of florida?<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||12-12-2000||12:48 PM||||Ed Parks says that most sunroof leaks are caused by leaking seals where the screws attach the latch and hinge plates to the glass.  The original ones had separate gaskets which eventually shrink and leak.  That's what happened to my 85SE.  I got the new screws which have integral rubber seals along with new hinge plates from Pontiac for under $20.00 US.  Problem solved.<P>Take a look at the sunroof seal (the rubber gasket surrounding the hole in the roof) to see if there are any obvious places where it does not seal against the glass solidly.  Close a piece of paper between the glass and the seal and see if it is free to move around (meaning that the glass is not tight against the rubber).<P>The hinge plates (for lack of a better name) are bolted to the front edge of the glass panel and stab into slots in the roof.  They are supposed to have a rubber like coating.  This coating had dried up and flaked off of mine which caused the glass to not be pulled down tight against the seal.  The glass also rattled when closed when I'd drive over rough roads.  The new hinge plates tightened this up.<P>You can also get a plastic spacer plate to mount between the rear latch mechanism and the roof structure to make the latch pull down tighter if needed.  I bought one from Pontiac but did not need it once I replaced the hinge plates and mounting screws.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||afRaceR||12-12-2000||06:21 PM||||Wheat's the black goop around the hardware in the glass. I took mine apart to tint the window and all this crap fell apart. Is this just silicon or tar? What can I use to replace this stuff?<P>Thanks for the tip on the hinge plates? I was trying to decide what I could recoat the hinges with.<P>I am also getting ready to replace the seal for the weatherstripping. I read some where some holes need to be drilled to allow water to drain. Does anyone have any pictures of where I need to drill? I don't want to mess this up.<BR>Thanks ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||12-12-2000||07:41 PM||||I believe the black goop is the remnants of the seal for the screws.  Get the new ones from the Pontiac dealer, they have an integral seal.  The new hinge plates and hardware may solve your leaking problem so you don't have to drill holes or replace the gasket.  For around $20 US it's worth a try before you drill any holes.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||afRaceR||12-12-2000||08:03 PM||||Thanks....I didn't have any leaking until I fooled around with this...I'll try that, I'm sure that will fix it. Thanks again||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||12-13-2000||01:53 AM||||one thing - the rails that the weather seal fits into can become rusted, especially around the rivits and screw holes -- the rails themselves are metal and are rivited to the roof. make sure that is all cleaned out before replacing the weather seal. Also, there is a drain hole in the rear corner of the channel on each side of the top - it is probably filled with dirt as they usually do over time. These drain holes allow for water to drain out along a channel down the side of the car and out from under the roof at the rear. Clean these good when replacing the weather seal. No need to drill holes, they are already there.......also, use silicone along the underneath and along the edges of the seal when you reinstall it.<P>As for the latches and front spade fasteners, if you can't find the rubber grommets you can always remove the torx screws and inject a small amount of silicone in the hole in the glass before reattaching the latch and spade fasteners......||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||12-13-2000||06:35 AM||||Neither my 85SE nor my 85GT have drain holes for the sunroof.  I believe they were used on later models.||||reg||1
Z||000007||overlord||12-13-2000||10:38 AM||||just a cheap fix if your hinges have the rubber coating worn off.<BR>I llooked at my hinges and all that black plastic or rubber stuff was gone, and the hinges were loose and wobbly......so i just used those rubber rings that u use with garden hoses and tightened em up real good. Problem solved....no leaking and the sunroof closes firmly now.||||reg||1
